Welcome back — a few quick things before we dive in.

requirements.txt question
Good instinct to ask, but it's not quite the right layer here. requirements.txt is a Python convention (from pip freeze) — for Node.js projects, package.json already is that file. It already lists every dependency with exact versions, and npm install reads it the same way pip install -r requirements.txt would. Adding a requirements.txt on top would be non-standard for a JS project and probably read as a mistake by the marker, not a bonus.

docs/third-party-code.md is doing a different job entirely: package.json is the machine-readable manifest (what to install), while third-party-code.md is the human-readable justification (why you chose each one) — which is what the rubric is actually grading. You need both, but they're not duplicates of each other.

