Ha — not too bad, but worth fixing properly rather than ignoring, since hydration mismatches can silently cause weird behavior later (React warns "this won't be patched up," meaning the DOM stays wrong).

What's actually happening
@dnd-kit auto-generates IDs like DndDescribedBy-0 for accessibility (screen readers announcing "press space to pick up this draggable item"). Each time a DndContext mounts, it increments an internal counter. The problem: your server-rendered HTML and the client's first render aren't guaranteed to hit that counter at the same value, so the server says DndDescribedBy-0 and the client says DndDescribedBy-1 — same content, different auto-generated ID, React flags it as a mismatch.

This is a known rough edge with @dnd-kit under Next.js's server-rendering, not something you did wrong.

The fix
DndContext accepts an id prop specifically to make this deterministic instead of relying on an internal counter:


Edited a file
Edited a file
Apply the same one-line change in your local TaskBoard.tsx:

tsx
<DndContext id="task-board" onDragEnd={handleDragEnd}>
That gives the accessibility IDs a fixed, deterministic value instead of an incrementing counter, so server and client agree every time.

After making that change, restart npm run dev and reload the page — the console error should be gone. If it's still there, paste the new error and I'll take another look; hydration warnings are worth chasing down fully rather than living with, since "won't be patched up" means React gives up reconciling that part of the tree for the rest of that session.

